{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310785,"nonce":"qzq3UeaEdesXDFh/SwxyJlAYx1c=","title":"2026-06-12 dispatch/threading session (commit 4082341) — caller-assist fork-join, QoS-pin orchestrator, GQA leak frees. AOT reached JIT ...","message":"Commit 4082341 (2026-06-12). Research workflow (pool audit + FFI census +\nggml comparison) + serialized benching via the SERVER_BIN harness.\n\n**Landed (combined effect: AOT 77.6 → 81.36, = JIT plateau 81.38):**\n1. `parallel_rows` caller participation (caller computes band 0;\n   `RAYZOR_NO_CALLER_BAND=1` reverts): +0.5% stable.\n2. `auto_kernel_threads()` — five sites in quant.rs/tensor.rs hardcoded\n   6 bands, so `RAYZOR_WORKERS` never widened matmul fan-out. Now follows\n   pool (+1 with caller assist). Default stays 6 workers = 7 compute.\n3. QoS-pin orchestrator thread at pool init (was: workers INTERACTIVE,\n   caller default-QoS → E-core demotion episodes). Together with #4 this\n   recovered ~3 tok/s — most of the apparent \"AOT slower than JIT\" gap.\n4. GQAttention frees: qRaw/kRaw after rope (rope allocs fresh), k/v\n   after cache.append (append copies), q + qByHead/kT views at all three\n   exits. Part of [[bugs_decode_loop_per_step_leak]] (~80 handles/token).\n\n**Decisive negative results (don't re-attempt without new conditions):**\n- Static 8-band fan-out + spinning caller = 9 runnable on 8 P-cores →\n  **2.3x decode collapse (33.9 tok/s)**, E-core straggler gates every\n  join. The old \"more threads doesn't help\" comment in quant.rs was\n  this, misdiagnosed.\n- Compute-width scaling is SATURATED: 6→7 compute +0.5%, 7→8 +0.1%\n  (clean runs) and fragile under static split (one 68.8 outlier when\n  the OS preempts any of 8 busy P-cores). ~59-69 GB/s weight streaming\n  is at practical DRAM limits for this pattern. Dynamic chunk stealing\n  would stabilize 8-wide but buys ~nothing median — below the line.\n- ggml-style per-layer mega-dispatch: demoted by washout-calibrated\n  join cost (~1-2μs marginal, not 5-12μs; 97 joins/token ≈ 0.15ms).\n  Kernel-timing run measured dispatch_wait ≈ work time + ~10μs/call —\n  the 10μs includes alloc/setup, not pure sync.\n\n**State of the gap:** rayzor 81.4 vs llama.cpp CPU ~87 (2026-06-07\nmeasurement) ≈ 6%. With compute width and dispatch overhead exhausted,\nremaining levers are bandwidth-side: smaller bytes/token streamed\n(activation quantization Q8_0 dot path like ggml's vec_dot_q4_K_q8_K\noperates int8×int8; we dequant activations to f32?? — verify), kernel\nmetadata layout, or Metal/GPU. Tiling: register-level already landed;\ncache-blocking has nothing to bite on for batch=1 GEMV (no reuse);\nprefill GEMM blocking is a TTFT lever, not tok/s.\n\n**Methodology:** DECODE_PROFILE / KERNEL_TIMING cost 2-7% — compare\nlike-for-like only. SERVER_BIN bench mode (b1ee887) drives prebuilt\nbinaries with the same harness. Scrub .rayzor after ANY compiler\nrebuild or NO_CACHE=false benches poison ([[bugs_rayzor_cache_scrub]]).\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `perf_dispatch_threading_arc` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}